assembly/ - the runtime in assembly
-----------------------------------

    yupiter_start_aarch64.S
                      Program entry point for Linux on aarch64.  The
                      kernel enters _start with argc, argv and envp on
                      the stack; the file clears the frame chain, calls
                      main(argc, argv, envp) and hands main's return
                      value to yupiter_sys_exit.  There are no
                      constructors, no ".init_array" walk and no atexit
                      handling: the freestanding startup path does not
                      provide them.  Documented in docs/io.
    yupiter_start_x86_64.S
                      The same for Linux on x86_64.  It also aligns
                      the stack to 16 bytes before the call, as the
                      System V AMD64 ABI requires.
    yupiter_syscall_aarch64.S
                      The six-argument system-call trampoline for
                      aarch64.  It converts the ordinary C calling
                      convention into the Linux system-call convention
                      (number in x8, arguments in x0..x5, result in x0)
                      with a single "svc #0".  Documented in docs/io.
    yupiter_syscall3_aarch64.S
                      The three-argument trampoline for read, write,
                      close and exit, split out so those calls do not
                      pay for the three extra argument moves.
                      Documented in docs/io.
    yupiter_syscall_x86_64.S
                      The six-argument trampoline for x86_64: the
                      number goes in rax, the fourth argument in r10,
                      and the kernel is entered with "syscall".
                      Documented in docs/io.
    yupiter_syscall3_x86_64.S
                      The three-argument form for x86_64: it avoids the
                      fourth, fifth and sixth argument moves, including
                      the load from the stack.  Documented in docs/io.


io/ - the input/output layer
----------------------------

    The sources here are ISO C++23.  Every function the headers declare
    is defined with C linkage, so the library exports the C interface
    and nothing else; the declarations live in include/yupiter_io.hxx.

    file.cxx          The three standard streams (yupiter_stdin,
                      yupiter_stdout, yupiter_stderr) and the staging
                      logic: yupiter_stream_write, yupiter_stream_flush
                      and yupiter_stream_discard.  A write that starts
                      with an empty buffer is handed to the kernel
                      directly when the stream is unbuffered or the
                      payload is larger than the staging buffer.  The
                      stream objects are "constinit", so they need no
                      dynamic initialization.  Documented in docs/io.
    vfprintf.cxx      The formatting engine, yupiter_vfprintf.  Parser
                      and formatter live in one translation unit.
                      Documented in docs/formatting.
    printf.cxx        yupiter_printf: the yupiter_stdout wrapper around
                      yupiter_vfprintf.
    fprintf.cxx       yupiter_fprintf: the same for a given stream.
    syscall_linux.cxx The operating-system wrappers
                      (yupiter_sys_write, yupiter_sys_read,
                      yupiter_sys_close, yupiter_sys_exit) and the only
                      place where Linux system-call numbers appear.
                      Documented in docs/io.
    string.cxx        The internal helpers yupiter_strlen, yupiter_memcpy
                      and yupiter_memset, which exist so that the
                      library links with "-nostdlib" without taking
                      symbols from a host C library.  The copy and
                      fill helpers move sixteen bytes per iteration;
                      the length helper stays byte-wise so it never
                      reads past the terminator.


mem/ - dynamic memory
---------------------

    heap.cxx          The allocator: a static arena, first-fit block
                      allocation with splitting and merging, resizing
                      in place or by moving, the safe variants that
                      terminate the process on a detected fault, and
                      the accounting the tests assert on.  The arena is
                      an array of "std::byte" and the block state is a
                      fixed-width scoped enumeration, so the on-arena
                      layout is the same on every target.  Documented in
                      docs/heap.


include/ - the headers
-----------------------

    yupiter.h         The C header: the version macros, the streams,
                      yupiter_printf, yupiter_fprintf and the dynamic
                      memory functions yupiter_malloc, yupiter_free,
                      yupiter_realloc, yupiter_malloc_safe,
                      yupiter_free_safe and yupiter_realloc_safe.  The
                      comments in this file are the contract of the
                      library.  It depends only on "<stdarg.h>" and
                      "<stddef.h>", so it works in a freestanding build.
                      Documented in docs/formatting and docs/heap.
    yupiter.hpp       The C++ entry point: it includes yupiter.h
                      inside an "extern "C"" specification so the
                      symbols are not mangled.  No C++ runtime is
                      required.
    yupiter_io.hxx    The internal interface shared by the I/O layer
                      and the platform layer: the stream structure,
                      YUPITER_BUFFER_SIZE and the internal function
                      declarations.  It is not installed, and it is
                      kept ISO C17 so the C tests can include it; the
                      declarations are wrapped in an "extern "C"" for
                      the C++23 implementation.
    yupiter_mem.hxx   The internal header of the allocator:
                      YUPITER_HEAP_SIZE and the accounting queries used
                      by the tests.  It is not installed and follows
                      the same rules as yupiter_io.hxx.


etc/ - installed package metadata
---------------------------------

    yupiter.pc.in     Template of the pkg-config file, filled in by the
                      build with the chosen prefix.
    yupiter-config.cmake.in
                      Template of the CMake package config, so a
                      consumer can "find_package(yupiter)".  The build
                      fills in the architecture, the library kind and
                      the relative installation directories.
    yupiter-config-version.cmake.in
                      Template of the CMake package version file, which
                      states the compatibility rule of the 1.x series.

    None of these files needs CMake to be installed on the build host:
    the templates are filled in with sed by mk/package.mk.


test/ - the test programs
-------------------------

    run-tests.sh      The test runner: builds nothing, runs every test
                      program and the three inspection scripts, prints
                      PASS or FAIL per check and exits non-zero when one
                      failed.
    check-freestanding.sh
                      Inspects the built archive and shared object: every
                      undefined symbol has to be "main" or a yupiter
                      symbol, no exported symbol may be outside the C
                      interface, no member may hold a section that runs
                      code before the program, and the shared object may
                      name no NEEDED library.
    install-test.sh   Stages "make install DESTDIR=...", checks the
                      installed files, and builds consumer/ against the
                      staged prefix with pkg-config and, when available,
                      with find_package(yupiter).
    consumer/         A small project that consumes the installed
                      package.  It is not part of the yupiter build.

    The programs:

    index.c, index.cxx
                      The original smoke test, once as C and once as
                      C++, proving the public header works from both.
                      The C++ one is a hosted program: a C++ program
                      needs the C++ runtime for its own start-up.  It
                      is compiled as C++17 on purpose, so the test
                      proves that consuming yupiter does not require
                      C++23.
    format.c          The formatting engine, against golden output a
                      reference C library produced from the same
                      source.
    differential.c    The same engine, compiled once against the host C
                      library and once against yupiter and compared
                      case by case, over cases built at run time.
    errors.c          The failure paths of the formatted functions.
    stream.c          The staging buffer, the discard and the order of
                      the two output streams.
    largewrite.c      One 200000 byte write through the system-call
                      layer, checked on a file and through a pipe.
    shortwrite.c      Replaces the three-argument trampoline for one
                      program, scripts short, empty, oversized and
                      failing writes, and proves that yupiter_sys_write
                      retries a short count until the buffer is sent.
    string.c          The internal length, copy and fill helpers,
                      including their null and zero-length cases.
    heap.c            The allocator, its safe variants and the leak
                      accounting.
    heap_abort.c      The fail-fast paths: one scenario per run,
                      selected on the command line.
    rawsyscall_aarch64.S, rawsyscall_x86_64.S
                      The real three-argument trampoline compiled into
                      shortwrite.c, so calls the mock does not script
                      still reach the kernel.  They are test-only copies
                      of assembly/yupiter_syscall3_<processor>.S.

    The golden files:

    expected-index.txt, expected-format.txt, expected-stream.txt
                      The output the programs are compared with.  The
                      first two were produced by a reference C library;
                      the third states a property that only yupiter
                      defines.  See docs/testing.

    The build rules for the programs live in mk/tests.mk, not in this
    directory.  The whole directory is documented in docs/testing.


mk/ - the Makefile modules
--------------------------

    variables.mk      Source and object lists, the warning sets, the
                      optimisation flags, the library names and the test
                      lists.  Every value derived from config.mk lives
                      here.
    rules.mk          The pattern rules that compile a .cxx or .S file
                      into an object and the dependency-file includes.
    library.mk        The static archive, the shared object and its two
                      symlinks.
    tests.mk          The test programs and the "check" target.
    package.mk        Fills in the pkg-config and CMake package
                      templates.
    install.mk        "install" and "uninstall", with DESTDIR support.

    config.mk is written by ./configure and is not part of the source:
    it records the target, the compilers, the prefix, the build type,
    the heap size and which libraries and tests to build.


.github/ - automation
---------------------

    dependabot.yml    Keeps the GitHub Actions dependencies updated.
    workflows/        The build and test matrix (aarch64 and x86_64,
                      GCC and Clang, Debug, RelWithDebInfo and Release,
                      static, shared and both), the golden-file
                      regeneration check and the configure guard.  See
                      docs/building.
    workflows/security.yml
                      clang-tidy, flawfinder and the dependency review.
    workflows/codeql.yml
                      The CodeQL analysis workflow.  None of these
                      files takes part in the build: this repository
                      builds with no network access.


Top-level files
---------------

    README            Project overview, repository structure and the
                      license summary.  Points here for details.
    NEWS              Release notes, newest release first: 1.0.0, the
                      first stable release under the yupiter name, then
                      the history of the project under its former name.
    ChangeLog         States that Git holds the detailed history.
    AUTHORS           The project founder, maintainer and developer.
    LICENSE           Explains the three licenses the recipient may
                      choose from.
    LICENSES/         GPL-2.0.txt, LGPL-2.1.txt, Apache-2.0.txt and a
                      README that explains how the choice works.
    AGENTS.md         The conventions that govern changes to this
                      tree: the language rules (C++23 implementation,
                      C17 interface), freestanding rules, style, the
                      priority of instructions.  Read it before
                      editing anything.


